# Keep the docker-compose build context small. Without this, the build
# context grows with every git worktree and every install artifact under
# this directory, which slows down every `docker compose build`.
.git
.worktrees/
.pnpm-store/
node_modules/
prep/
.env

# Ticket 17d14b1, opus review F7: the patterns above were written for the
# dev compose file (repo-root Dockerfile) and had two gaps that only
# matter now that this file is also load-bearing for PRODUCTION images
# (deploy/api.Dockerfile, deploy/web.Dockerfile):
#
#   - `node_modules/` and a bare `.env` only match the TOP LEVEL of the
#     build context, not `apps/api/node_modules`, `apps/web/node_modules`,
#     `packages/shared/node_modules`, or `.env.prod` -- every one of those
#     can exist locally (a worktree that already ran `pnpm install`, or
#     `deploy/README.md`'s own `.env.prod` for the self-host path) and, if
#     present, gets copied into the build context and `COPY . .`'d into
#     the build stage regardless. `.env`/`.env.prod` there means real
#     secrets (a real POSTGRES_PASSWORD, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY) enter the
#     build layer even though the runtime stage's explicit COPY list never
#     reads them back out.
#   - a stray per-package `node_modules` copied into the build context can
#     also be a LEFTOVER, differently-versioned tree from a previous local
#     install -- `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` inside the image should
#     still repair it correctly (it always resolves against the lockfile,
#     not whatever's already on disk), but there's no reason to ship that
#     risk or the wasted context size for free.
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
.env*
!.env.example
